WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Multiple observatories have combined forces to reveal the tarantula nebula's turbulent star

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:13.040
birth in unprecedented color and detail. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 168, a new

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:19.460
space transportation policy directs agencies to enable more than 1,000 launches and re-entries annually

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:26.840
by 2030. The memorandum focuses on streamlining regulatory processes and expanding ground infrastructure

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:32.880
without specifying funding levels. Industry groups are now assessing how quickly capacity can

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.880
grow under the stated national priority. On a completely different note, Europe has dropped plans to

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.440
improve the Arianne's six rockets' performance and production rate. The decision leaves the vehicle

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:50.620
at its current target of roughly 10 flights per year with no revised roadmap released. Officials

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:56.540
have not announced any new steps to raise that cadence despite growing commercial demand. A single

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:02.180
Raptor engine fired on the ground to demonstrate the deorbit maneuver planned for future orbital flights.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.540
The test confirms the vehicle can perform the burn needed to return from orbit safely and engineers

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:14.240
will review the data before integrating the sequence into upcoming missions. This incremental step

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:20.460
moves Starship closer to routine orbital operations and adds one data point to its return profile.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:26.140
Remember, we covered Starship development yesterday and today's test advances the open

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:31.860
question of orbital refueling and rapid reuse cadence without yet addressing crude timelines.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.180
Jaxa confirmed an October 20 launch date for its probe to Phobos and Demos. The spacecraft will

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.420
collect surface material and return it to Earth, marking the first dedicated attempt to bring back

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.040
samples from either Martian moon. Scientists hope the material will clarify whether the moons are

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:57.840
captured asteroids or fragments of Mars itself. China prepares the Chung E7 lander rover and hopper for

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.780
an August 24 launch toward the lunar South Pole. The mission will deploy multiple instruments to search

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.660
for water ice and study local geology with a communications relay satellite supporting operations

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:16.220
from lunar orbit. Success would mark China's most complex lunar surface campaign to date and target

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:22.540
permanently shadowed regions where ice may persist. This development fits the ongoing Artemis and moon

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:28.560
arc by testing multi-vehicle operations at the South Pole where landing site selection and resource

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:35.120
questions remain open. Laboratory tests show certain terrestrial organisms survive conditions that

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.800
mimic permanently shadowed lunar craters. The study suggests microbes could endure in thin

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.740
films of water ice protected from solar radiation and researchers note that even small disturbances

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:52.860
such as rover tracks might create temporary habitable pockets. Future missions will need to weigh

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:59.220
contamination risks against scientific goals. Hubble, Chandra, and Web data together show the

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:06.480
Tarantula Nebula's star-forming region invisible x-ray and infrared light at 160,000 light years away

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.540
in the large Magellanic cloud. The composite highlights stellar winds carving through dense gas

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.620
and dust, and NASA described the result as a vibrant look at one of the Milky Way's neighboring galaxies.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.160
The combined data set spans wavelengths that single observatories cannot capture alone.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.520
Astrobiologists ran fresh simulations that begin with different initial conditions than earlier studies

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:35.860
and produced solar system analogues with rocky planets in stable orbits more often than

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:41.820
previous attempts. The work indicates our architecture is not an outlier under realistic starting

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.880
assumptions that incorporate updated disk chemistry and dynamical interactions. Follow-up papers will

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:54.560
test the models against observed exoplanet populations. New analysis indicates a single large collision

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.800
excavated demoses prominent South Polar Crater and left a thick regolith blanket across its surface.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.240
The moon's low density and porous interior suggest it behaves like a rubble pile rather than a solid

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:13.740
body, helping explain why smaller craters appear buried. The findings imply the moon could re-accrete

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:19.140
after major collisions, leaving its surface in a state of slow, ongoing rearrangement.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:25.000
A single 0.3 second exposure captured the August 12th partial solar eclipse,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:32.680
setting behind PISA's cathedral complex at 94% obscuration. The image places the leaning tower and

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:38.720
domes in silhouette against the dimmed solar disk, and such ground-based records complement satellite

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:45.160
observations of the event. Engineers have started constructing the Emilia 3D instrument for a future

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.660
lunar mission to map terrain and subsurface structure using three-dimensional imaging techniques.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.920
Data will support landing site selection and resource prospecting near the South Pole,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.420
mark on the university's first hardware contribution to a lunar flight mission.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:06.080
The AstroNAV instrument is being developed to give spacecraft real-time position fixes

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.280
without ground tracking by using star and planet observations during lunar and deep space cruises.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:18.180
Jet propulsion laboratory leads the project, which remains in the technology demonstration phase

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.520
and would reduce reliance on Earth-based navigation networks if successful.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:28.500
California's startup ELV has completed spaceflight qualification of its 100-watt

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.420
traveling wave tube amplifier, developed under a U.S. space for small business innovation research

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:40.120
contract. The component operates in the millimeter wave band, and will support high data-rate

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.740
communications on future small satellites, with additional units now in production.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:50.620
Two new educational packages feature interviews with Helio Physicists, Dr. Richard Fisher,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:56.640
and Dr. James Elgreen, tracing the evolution of solar monitoring and the discovery of space-weather

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.240
effects on technology. Teachers can use the material for units on magnetism and the Sun Earth

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.540
connection through NASA's learning portal. Elon Musk stated the first attempt to catch a

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:13.260
returning starship upper stage with the launch tower will occur within a few months of late 2026.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:19.400
The maneuver requires precise control of the booster's descent and the tower's mechanical arms,

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:24.040
and a successful catch would eliminate the need for landing legs on future vehicles.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:30.120
Remember, we covered starship development yesterday, and today's timeline revision moves

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.640
the reusability question forward while the open issue of rapid reuse cadence stays active.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.860
China's Chiang E7 mission will attempt the first landing near the lunar south pole

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:46.720
with a full suite of surface vehicles. A stationary lander will anchor the mission while a rover

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:52.840
traverses nearby terrain and a hopper leaps between shadowed craters, deploying instruments to measure

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:58.820
water ice signatures and local radiation levels. Success would give China operational experience

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:03.660
at one of the most scientifically valuable sites on the moon and set the stage for longer

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.780
duration surface operations with a dedicated relay satellite maintaining contact from orbit.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:15.400
Daymost measures only about 12 kilometers across, yet one ancient collision appears to have

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:21.580
reset much of its surface. Imagine a city size boulder striking a loosely bound rubble pile at

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:26.120
several kilometers per second. The shockwave travels through the porous interior,

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:30.740
lofting regolith that settles into a blanket thick enough to bury older craters.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:35.960
The same event excavates the prominent south polar base and now visible from orbit,

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:42.000
and because the moon's bulk density sits well below that of solid rock, the impact energy dissipates

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.700
quickly rather than shattering the body completely. Space craft imagery already maps the resulting

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:52.980
crater and regolith distribution, but we still do not know how many such events a rubble pile

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:59.220
satellite can absorb before a disperses or reacreats into a new shape. The low density also means

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:04.360
the moon's gravity is too weak to hold on to fine material ejected during later small impacts,

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.540
leaving the surface in a state of slow, ongoing rearrangement.

